{"iter": 3, "id": "c59ef2f7", "parent_id": "fcb3faae", "operation": "improve", "status": "closed", "evidence_type": "not_significant", "code_dir": "C:\\Users\\Ray\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\candidate_c59ef2f7", "hypothesis": {"mechanism": "Because pure BPR directly targets GAUC but can neglect top-five calibration and users that cannot form mixed-label pairs, alternating BPR updates with the existing pointwise loss should preserve nDCG@5 while adding within-user ranking pressure, producing a larger primary gain than either objective alone.", "success_criterion_paired": "Primary on val-tier-1 (the standard held-out validation split) improves by at least +0.0025 over the parent node's primary on val-tier-1, and neither GAUC nor nDCG@5 decreases relative to the parent on that same tier.", "implementation_sketch": "Edit baseline.py only. Add the same vectorized same-user positive/negative sampler used for BPR, but retain the current pointwise minibatch code. During each epoch alternate one BPR minibatch and one pointwise minibatch, or equivalently optimize L = alpha * L_BPR + (1-alpha) * L_BCE with shared FM parameters and Adam state. Evaluate a fixed small grid alpha in {0.25, 0.5, 0.75}; keep the total number of scored rows per epoch approximately equal across settings so improvements cannot be attributed only to extra optimization. Use one negative per positive, no pair enumeration, and select alpha solely by exact validation primary. As an exposure-bias guard, also report the candidate-minus-parent primary delta on the separately named val-tier-random random-exposure split, but do not train on that split."}, "diagnosis": {"bottleneck": "The FM is optimized with pointwise log loss rather than a within-user ranking loss aligned with GAUC and nDCG@5.", "evidence": "The branch remains essentially at the plain-FM baseline, improving primary by only 0.00056, while the model is trained independently per impression even though both evaluation metrics depend on ordering candidat