{"iter": 6, "id": "823c887c", "parent_id": "5b7ba11d", "operation": "improve", "status": "closed", "evidence_type": "not_significant", "code_dir": "C:\\Users\\Ray\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\candidate_823c887c", "hypothesis": {"mechanism": "Because positive-proportional BPR can let high-activity users dominate even though nDCG@5 averages users equally, uniformly sampling mixed-label users and capping the number of pairs contributed by each user should preserve ranking-aligned learning while improving top-five quality for the typical user.", "success_criterion_paired": "Primary on val-tier-1 improves by at least +0.0015 over the parent node's primary on val-tier-1, with nDCG@5 improving by at least +0.0015 and GAUC decreasing by no more than 0.0005 on the same tier.", "implementation_sketch": "Edit baseline.py only and use the same vectorized BPR gradient as the first hypothesis, but change pair construction. Build positive and negative row arrays grouped by user, retain only mixed-label users, and sample a fixed cap such as min(4, positive_count) positive-negative pairs per selected user per epoch. Shuffle users rather than all positive rows so each eligible user has approximately equal epoch weight. Implement sampling from group offsets with NumPy random integer arrays and benchmark caps of 2 and 4 on train plus validation, selecting only by val-tier-1 primary."}, "diagnosis": {"bottleneck": "The FM is bottlenecked by its pointwise log-loss objective, which does not directly optimize within-user ranking.", "evidence": "The running best has nearly plateaued, improving only 0.00090 over the last three-iteration window, while the model is still trained pointwise despite GAUC and nDCG@5 depending entirely on within-user ordering; no evidence indicates that pairwise or listwise FM losses have been tested.", "confidence": 0.72, "component": "loss_function", "edit_radius": "small", "expected_cost": "A localized baseline.py change implementing vectorized within-user BPR negative pairing or grouped softmax, followed by validation of sampling and learning-rate settings; feasible within the CPU budget.", "incompatibilities": ["Continuing to optimize only independent per-row pointwise log-loss", "Sampling negatives without preserving within-user comparisons"], "uncertainty": 0.28, "audit_concerns": ["test_label_access: ysel from the caller-controlled select_on split is read repeatedly for evaluation and model/cap selection; the diff does not establish that select_on can never be an unlabeled test/scoring split."]}, "per_seed_primary": {"0": 0.5848366022109985, "1": 0.5832818746566772, "2": 0.5843850374221802}, "per_seed_gauc": {"0": 0.6491739153862, "1": 0.6468826532363892, "2": 0.6489997506141663}, "per_seed_ndcg5": {"0": 0.5204992890357971, "1": 0.5196810364723206, "2": 0.5197703242301941}, "mean_delta": -0.010914466464720642, "p_positive": 0.0, "lower_95": -0.012845974022397651, "fix_attempts": 0, "wallclock_used_s": 0.0, "per_user_by_seed": {"0": {"4": 1.0, "15": 0.606574, "16": 0.932083, "21": 0.5, "23": 0.5, "25": 0.608217, "27": 0.448661, "29": 0.583333, "35": 1.0, "36": 1.0, "39": 1.0, "41": 0.688608, "45": 1.0, "46": 0.756783, "49": 0.776063, "50": 0.557027, "53": 0.284574, "54": 1.0, "55": 0.562025, "57": 1.0, "58": 0.958333, "60": 0.315465, "64": 0.341901, "65": 0.527838, "67": 1.0, "68": 0.527838, "71": 0.340338, "73": 0.38479, "74": 1.0, "75": 0.817067, "79": 1.0, "81": 0.834397, "82": 0.616414, "83": 1.0, "84": 0.315465, "94": 0.315465, "95": 1.0, "97": 1.0, "98": 0.565465, "99": 1.0, "102": 0.679676, "110": 1.0, "111": 0.382034, "112": 0.25, "113": 1.0, "114": 0.648798, "118": 0.616414, "121": 0.88023, "122": 0.542176, "124": 0.57546, "125": 0.564968, "127": 1.0, "129": 0.315465, "132": 0.45046, "138": 0.838608, "142": 0.383731, "147": 1.0, "149": 0.60134, "150": 0.416667, "152": 0.174202, "153": 1.0, "157": 0.218599, "158": 0.89736, "159": 0.346713, "160": 0.63042, "161": 1.0, "165": 1.0, "166": 0.223427, "168": 0.928946, "169